{
 "about": "Shared vectors for the wire encoding and its channel frames (docs/encodings.md, 'Values on the wire'). Every implementation reads this file: the Rust code, the C functions and each binding. The bytes were written from the grammar by an encoder of their own, not by the code under test. A tree is [tag] for absent and null, [tag, x] for a bool, a number (its text), a string, bytes (hex), a list (its items) and a map (its [key, tree] pairs, in order). 'values' decode to their tree and encode to their bytes; 'malformed' are refused with the byte offset; 'frames' are what announce, send and close write; 'conversation' runs through one receiver in order, each frame answering an event (with its channel and, for a schema or a value, its tree) or a refusal (malformed, no_schema, not_a_schema, invalid) with its channel, offset, frame kind or the refused field's key where given.",
